SHAMSU — elision poisoning, and per-session telemetry
=====================================================
2026-08-22 · branch small-shamsu-new
Diagnosed from a live session the user reported:
  F:\Work\demo2\asteroid-shamsu  session 20260820-055706-f20c  qwen3.5:9b-q4_K_M


THE REPORT
----------
Two symptoms after resuming a session:
  1. `/context budget` and `/context meter` said "No model calls made yet this
     session" while the model plainly had the history.
  2. At ctx 71% (23.3k/32.8k) the 9B collapsed: elision notices, failed patches
     (`old_string not found`, `old_string and new_string are identical`), and
     three rounds of talking without calling a tool.

The user's hypothesis was context pressure, to be fixed by Artifact Fix 08
(history compaction). Half right - see the assessment at the end.


1. THE POISONING — the actual mechanism
---------------------------------------
Not pressure. A string-truncation bug that fires whenever elision touches a
patch argument, at any context level.

    MAX_OLD_ARGUMENT_CHARS = 100
    OLD_ARGUMENT_KEEP_CHARS = 80
    value[:80] + " ...[elided]"

`_shorten_arguments` shortens the arguments of the model's OWN past tool calls
in the transcript. A `patch_file` `old_string` is essentially always over 100
chars, so every past patch was shown back to the model as an 80-char stub
ending in a literal ` ...[elided]`.

The model then retried by copying that stub. From the session's own
`log-detailed.md` - the same `fireWeapon` body, three times:

    L1962  old_string = "    /**\n     * Fire weapon - create projectile at ship
                          position moving forward\n    fireWeapon() { ...full..."
           -> old_string not found

    L2220  the same full text again
           -> fails

    L2873  old_string = "    /**\n     * Fire weapon - create projectile at ship
                          position moving forward\n  ...[elided]"
           -> old_string not found

The third attempt carries the harness's own marker as if it were code. It could
never have matched.

HONEST ACCOUNTING, because it is not everything:
    10 patch attempts, 4 failures
     2 carry the elision marker                 <- this bug
     1 "identical"  - both strings complete at 714 chars, a genuine no-op
     1 "not found"  - no marker, a stale copy

So this is HALF the patch failures. Claiming all four would have been wrong.


THE FIX — two halves, because either alone leaks
------------------------------------------------
a) Stop producing copyable stubs. `CONTENT_ARGUMENT_KEYS` = {old_string,
   new_string, content, body, text} are now DROPPED, not trimmed:

       patch_file(filepath=js/PlayerShip.js,
                  old_string=<omitted from history - call read_file for the
                              current text>)

   The keys stay, so the call still reads as an action rather than a hole -
   which was the original rationale and is preserved. It is the VALUES that had
   to go. A trimmed value reads like the code it came from; a missing one
   cannot be copied at all.

   Non-content keys are still merely trimmed. A path or a command cut mid-way
   is obviously truncated and nobody tries to run it.

b) Refuse one on the way in. `edit_file`/`patch_file` now checks `old_string`
   and `new_string` for either marker (the new one and the legacy
   ` ...[elided]`, because transcripts written before this fix are on disk and
   still resume) and returns:

       old_string is a fragment of the conversation history, not file text - it
       still carries the marker this harness puts in place of an old payload.
       Call read_file on <path> and copy the text from the result, not from
       your earlier message.

   Caught here rather than left to fail as "not found", because the two answers
   send the model to different places: "not found" reads as a stale copy and it
   retries the same text - which is exactly what happened three times.

   The contract lives in `agent_tools.py`, not the chat loop: `simple_chat`
   already imports `agent_tools`, so the reverse would cycle, and it is the
   tool that has to refuse.

VERIFIED against the exact string from the log:

    poisoned old_string -> REFUSED
       old_string is a fragment of the conversation history, not file text...
      data: {'elided_argument': 'old_string'}

    genuine edit -> applied
    file now: return this.ready;


2. THE TELEMETRY — two different bugs behind one symptom
--------------------------------------------------------
`/context budget` and `/context meter` do not read the same thing.

  budget -> `mgr.last_result`, the context manager's last compile. None on a
            fresh process. The message was literally true and useless.

  meter  -> `SESSION_COUNTERS`, a MODULE-LEVEL global. Its own docstring says
            "per session". It was per PROCESS:

              - cold after restart, which is what the user saw
              - never reset on `/sessions resume`, so running a turn in session
                A and resuming B made B report A's numbers as its own

            The second is worse than cold: not stale, WRONG, and silent.

The comment justified process-wide because a fresh loop is built per user
message. That is an argument against per-LOOP; it is not one for per-PROCESS.

FIXED:
  - `_COUNTERS_BY_SESSION` keyed by session id, with an explicit active pointer
    set by the loop when a turn starts AND by the REPL on `/sessions resume`.
    `SESSION_COUNTERS` is now a proxy onto the active session's counters, so
    the three surfaces that read the bare name keep working. Contaminating one
    session with another's numbers is now structurally impossible.

        in A: calls = 7
        resume B: calls = 0     (was reporting 7 before this fix)
        back to A: calls = 7    - not lost

  - `/context meter` on a cold resume estimates from the transcript on disk
    rather than reporting nothing. Against the user's own session:

        thread on disk : 323 messages, ~165,531 tokens
        window         : 32,768 tokens

        This thread is 5.1x the window. Only the most recent messages that fit
        are sent; the rest are elided every call. A thread several times the
        window spends most of each prompt re-sending history.

    Deliberately NOT rendered as a percentage. My first version printed
    "165,531 / 32,768 (505%)", which is alarming and wrong - an archive larger
    than the window is normal, `select_for_budget` carries what fits. The ratio
    is the useful number and it is also the diagnosis: at 5.1x, most of every
    prompt is history being re-sent, which is the condition elision fires under.

  - `/context status` showed `ctx 256k` for the 9B while the turn ran at 32k
    and degraded, with nothing on screen explaining the gap. Now:

        qwen3.5:9b-q4_K_M     ctx 32k (of 256k, capped)  roles: router, qa, ...

        Chat runs at most 32k however large the model's own window is.
        Ollama reserves the KV cache for the WHOLE num_ctx up front, so the
        window is VRAM you have already spent - measured on an 8GB card, 32k
        fits on the GPU with a q8_0 cache and f16 does not.


ASSESSMENT: IS FIX 08 THE ANSWER?
---------------------------------
Right fix for the COST, wrong layer for the CORRECTNESS.

The cost is real: this turn was 51 model calls, 22.5 minutes of model time, and
15 reads of `js/PlayerShip.js`, to end `✗ failed`. Fix 08 attacks exactly that.

But the poisoning fires whenever elision touches a patch argument. Fix 08
lowers pressure, so elision fires less often - it makes the bug RARER, never
impossible, and it would return the first time someone edits a large file.

There is also a measurement trap, which is why the order matters: ship Fix 08
first and the failure rate drops, and nothing tells you how much was compaction
and how much was simply fewer elisions. With the poison gone, Fix 08's benefit
is measurable.


